Source: restate
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Bryan C. Everly <bryan@theeverlys.com>
# Deliberately short. restate links nothing but libc: there is nothing to
# vendor and nothing to fetch, and a Launchpad builder -- which has no
# network -- builds it from the source package alone. What it runs (gzip,
# gpgv, gpg, curl, pigz) it runs as separate processes, so those are runtime
# dependencies, below, and none of them is needed to build.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/bceverly/restate
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/bceverly/restate.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/bceverly/restate
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: restate
# A compiled C program, so tied to an architecture; "any", because nothing in
# it is particular to one.
Architecture: any
# gpgv is a Depends: restore refuses an image whose signature it cannot check,
# and gpgv is what checks it. It is priority important rather than essential,
# so a minimal system can be without it. gzip and tar are Essential, and so
# not named: depending on one is a Policy violation lintian reports.
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 gpgv,
# gpg encrypts and signs images (--encrypt-to, --sign-with); curl fetches
# installers and the package baseline; pigz compresses on every core. restate
# says plainly which is missing when it needs one.
Recommends:
 curl,
 gpg,
 pigz,
# dpkg-repack rebuilds a .deb that apt's cache no longer holds, for
# capture --keep-local-packages.
Suggests:
 dpkg-repack,
Description: record what makes a machine different from a fresh install
 restate records what makes a machine different from a fresh install of its
 operating system, and puts that difference back after a reinstall: onto the
 same machine, a new one, or a virtual machine.
 .
 Every file is classified: what the system regenerates, what is safe to lose,
 what the installed packages provide unmodified, and the state that makes
 the machine itself -- configuration changed from what its package shipped,
 and the files no package owns. Only that state is kept. The packages are
 recorded instead -- each at its version, with the repositories and keys it
 came from -- along with the disk layout, LUKS, LVM and RAID, and the
 machine's hardware.
 .
 From that record restate writes a build sheet and an Ubuntu autoinstall
 file to rebuild the machine, fetches the installer and the exact packages,
 verified against their vendors' signatures, and restores: the packages
 first, from the archive or with no network at all, then the files, each
 checked against the image's signed index.
 .
 Captures can go into a repository that stores each file once, encrypted
 if wanted, with snapshots pruned on a schedule. Databases and virtual
 machines are paused for exactly as long as their own files take to copy.
 .
 One C program that links only libc.
